    "body": "Adds ADR-014, which decides how the browser ships default styles and makes the cascade origin-aware.\n\n## The gap this fixes\n\nAfter v0.1.0, an unstyled HTML page still looks nothing like its Firefox rendering. `<h1>Title</h1>` is 16px (same as `<p>`), `<p>` has no paragraph gap, `<body>` has no margin. Every real browser ships a UA stylesheet that gives these elements their expected defaults -- authors write their CSS against that baseline.\n\n## The decision\n\nTwo coordinated changes:\n\n1. Ship a UA stylesheet as a first-class module (`renderer/css/ua_stylesheet.{hpp,cpp}`). ~20 rules covering the elements layout already renders.\n\n2. Make the cascade origin-aware: `cascade(ua_sheet, author_sheet, doc)`. For normal declarations, author rules beat UA rules **regardless of specificity**. Per CSS 2.1 section 6.4.1.\n\n## Why origin-aware over concatenation\n\nThe naive implementation is `Stylesheet::parse(ua_css + author_css)`. This breaks on a specific case:\n\n- UA: `h1 { color: navy }` (specificity 0,0,1)\n- Author: `* { color: red }` (specificity 0,0,0)\n\nConcatenation: `beats()` checks specificity first, so author's (0,0,0) loses to UA's (0,0,1). The h1 stays navy. Wrong per spec.\n\nOrigin-aware: author origin beats UA origin before specificity is checked. The h1 becomes red. Correct.\n\nThis case matters in practice -- `* { box-sizing: border-box }` and similar reset rules are very common.\n\n## Why this ADR is possible now\n\n`h2 { font-size: 1.5em }` in a UA stylesheet requires correct `em` resolution against parent font-size. ADR-013 put font-size computation in layout, threading parent font-size through recursion. Without that, `font-size: 1.5em` would compute against the wrong reference. This ADR is possible because ADR-013 landed.\n\n## Scope of v1\n\n~20 rules: body (margin 8px), h1-h6 (font-size + margin + bold), p (margin 1em 0), em/i/cite (italic), strong/b (bold), code/pre (monospace), ul/ol (margin + padding-left), a (blue).\n\n## Deferred\n\n- display inline/none (layout is block-only)\n- Retiring the `<head>` skip list (requires `display: none` in layout)\n- Table elements, form elements, list markers\n- text-decoration on links (paint doesn't support it)\n- Pseudo-class selectors (`a:link`, `a:visited`)\n- UA `!important` rules\n- Full CSS 2.1 UA stylesheet (~100 rules)\n\n## Consequences\n\n- New files: `src/renderer/css/ua_stylesheet.{hpp,cpp}`, `tests/test_ua_stylesheet.cpp`\n- `src/renderer/css/cascade.{hpp,cpp}` -- Origin enum, new signature, beats() change\n- `src/browser/render_pipeline.cpp` -- pass ua_stylesheet() first\n- No changes to `renderer::dom`, `renderer::layout`, `renderer::paint`, `renderer::html`\n\n## Expected visible effect\n\nAfter the implementation lands, an unstyled `<h1>Title</h1>` becomes 32px with margin above/below. Unstyled `<p>` gets paragraph gaps. Unstyled `<body>` gets an 8px inset. Every page looks meaningfully more like its Firefox rendering, even with zero author CSS.\n\n## Next\n\nOnce ADR-014 merges, the implementation issue will be \"Implement origin-aware cascade and UA stylesheet per ADR-014\" -- one atomic commit (both halves ship together, since either alone breaks tests), same rhythm as #116.\n",
